/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_Toolchains Toolchains
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Configuration
 * @brief CMake cross-compilation configuration and adding new targets.
 *
 * Native desktop builds generally use host compilers selected by their preset.
 * Cross-compiled targets use a CMake toolchain file to describe the target
 * system, compiler programs, architecture flags, ABI, and find-root behavior.
 * CMake loads the toolchain before `project()`, so changing a compiler, target
 * processor, or sysroot requires a fresh cache.
 *
 * Both repository cross-toolchains set `CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=Generic` and
 * `CMAKE_TRY_COMPILE_TARGET_TYPE=STATIC_LIBRARY`. The latter prevents compiler
 * detection from requiring startup objects or a linker script. It does not make
 * a complete firmware: the consuming project still owns startup, memory layout,
 * runtime initialization, linker script, and board/device integration.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowArmToolchain GNU Arm Embedded
 *
 * The generic toolchain is `cmake/toolchains/arm-none-eabi.cmake`. Reusable
 * profiles in `cmake/toolchains/arm-none-eabi/` supply CPU and floating-point
 * defaults for Cortex-M0+, M3, M4F, and M7F.
 *
 * Configurable cache values include:
 *
 * - `FOUNDATION_ARM_TOOLCHAIN_ROOT`: optional installation root whose `bin`
 *   directory contains the compiler programs;
 * - `FOUNDATION_ARM_TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX`: compiler executable prefix, normally
 *   `arm-none-eabi`;
 * - `FOUNDATION_ARM_CPU`: value passed through `-mcpu`;
 * - `FOUNDATION_FLOAT_ABI`: `soft`, `softfp`, or `hard`;
 * - `FOUNDATION_FPU`: value passed through `-mfpu` when applicable;
 * - `FOUNDATION_ARM_SYSROOT`: target C runtime headers and libraries;
 * - `FOUNDATION_ARM_ADDITIONAL_FLAGS`: extra C and C++ target flags.
 *
 * Hard-float profiles require an explicit FPU. All profiles use Thumb mode,
 * section-level garbage collection support, and disable C++ exceptions and RTTI.
 *
 * | Profile | CPU flag | Float ABI | FPU |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `cortex-m0plus-soft` | `cortex-m0plus` | `soft` | none |
 * | `cortex-m3-soft` | `cortex-m3` | `soft` | none |
 * | `cortex-m4f-hard` | `cortex-m4` | `hard` | `fpv4-sp-d16` |
 * | `cortex-m7f-hard` | `cortex-m7` | `hard` | `fpv5-d16` |
 *
 * Profile files assign CPU/ABI/FPU with `CACHE ... FORCE`; those values are
 * target invariants. Create a new profile instead of overriding them from a
 * command line. `FOUNDATION_ARM_SYSROOT` and additional flags remain
 * machine/product inputs and may be configured externally.
 *
 * @warning The compiler alone may not provide target runtime headers. If a build
 * fails to locate headers such as `stdint.h`, install the matching Newlib/runtime
 * package or configure `FOUNDATION_ARM_SYSROOT` to a compatible SDK sysroot.
 * Compiler, runtime, CPU, FPU, and float ABI must all agree.
 * The toolchain searches `arm-none-eabi-gcc`, `arm-none-eabi-g++`,
 * `arm-none-eabi-ar`, and `arm-none-eabi-ranlib`. Place their directory on
 * `PATH` or configure `FOUNDATION_ARM_TOOLCHAIN_ROOT`. Verify the actual tools
 * selected in CMake's configure summary.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowAVRToolchain AVR-GCC
 *
 * `cmake/toolchains/avr-gcc.cmake` provides the reusable AVR-GCC layer. Device
 * profiles must set `FOUNDATION_AVR_MCU` and `FOUNDATION_AVR_ARCHITECTURE`.
 * The initial `cmake/toolchains/avr-gcc/atmega328p.cmake` profile selects the
 * ATmega328P and AVR5 architecture. `FOUNDATION_AVR_TOOLCHAIN_ROOT` may point
 * to an installation whose `bin` directory is not on `PATH`.
 *
 * The profile fixes `-mmcu=atmega328p` and reports `avr5` as the system
 * processor. The common toolchain also enables function/data sections, garbage
 * collection at executable link time, and disables exceptions and RTTI.
 * `FOUNDATION_AVR_ADDITIONAL_FLAGS` is available for product-specific compiler
 * flags, but an MCU change requires a new profile and preset.
 *
 * AVR-GCC distributions vary in supported C++ language/library functionality.
 * Foundation requires C++17 language compilation but avoids a dependency on the
 * general C++ standard library. Validate each compiler version and real consumer
 * rather than assuming every AVR package is equivalent.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowToolchainInspect Inspecting Cross Outputs
 *
 * `build/<preset>/compile_commands.json` shows the effective per-source flags.
 * Confirm that every C++ command has the expected CPU/device and ABI flags.
 * Inspect installed archives with the target binutils:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * arm-none-eabi-objdump -f dist/<arm-preset>/lib/libFoundation.a
 * avr-objdump -f dist/atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5/lib/libFoundation.a
 * @endcode
 *
 * Archive inspection proves object architecture, not full firmware
 * compatibility. The final consumer must use the same runtime and ABI.
 *
 * @section FoundationWorkflowAddTarget Adding a Target
 *
 * 1. Reuse an existing profile or add a focused toolchain file under
 *    `cmake/toolchains/`.
 * 2. Add a configure preset under `cmake/presets/` and select its
 *    `toolchainFile` or a focused profile that includes the generic toolchain.
 * 3. Add one target-identity configure preset and matching build/test presets.
 *    Select Debug or Release as a build configuration, not in the preset name.
 * 4. Set `FOUNDATION_PLATFORM` to the artifact identity and validate the
 *    processor or ABI when CMake can report it.
 * 5. Add the preset file to the include graph in `CMakePresets.json`.
 * 6. Configure, build, export, and verify the resulting library architecture
 *    with the repository scripts.
 * 7. Compile a separate consumer with `find_package(Foundation CONFIG)` against
 *    the exported prefix before publishing it.
 * 8. Document prerequisites, generated flags, limitations, and acceptance steps.
 *
 * No platform-specific branch should be added to the scripts. New target logic
 * belongs in the preset and toolchain configuration so every workflow can use it.
 */
